The milestone 6 check-pass plan matches the engine. TDD steps are already test-then-code, and none is a change-detector, so tasks.md was not edited.

`copy_warehouse` leaves `warehouse.copy.duckdb` on success and deletes it, and its `.wal`, on any exception. It does not take the writer flock. `generations_file` is a `v1.0.0` file at schema 9, so it is not already new and the alarm child does reach a patched `copy_warehouse`. `released_chain_file(8)` is below `FORMAT_BOUNDARY`. `_flock` is an exclusive lock on one open file description: a second open in the same process fails with `BlockingIOError`, which is what the busy test relies on. `storage_facts` has four returns (absent, busy, unavailable, present). The probe help text is the description on `python -m stockroom.doctor`, and `probe --help` prints that same description. The preparation comment to extend is the `stockroom doctor probe` line. One short zstd row is enough for the existing segment check to pass. The non-zstd recipe, including a messages row that omits `text`, produces a persistent `DICT_FSST` segment and is not already new, as long as the helper keeps `_require_zstd`'s segment loop.

Do not treat "empty columns return None" as a skip of that loop. A column with no non-null values can still have a persistent segment. On this DuckDB, removing the zstd clause and inserting a messages row with only `ordinal` and `role` leaves `messages.text` null and still stores `(VARCHAR, DICT_FSST)`. The loop reports it. Returning None whenever the non-null count is 0 marks that fixture already new, and the non-zstd test fails.

An unexpired `started` record is not a live pass. `run_check` takes the flock before it reads the record. The process that wrote `started` has already released the flock, or this pass would still be waiting on it. Leaving the record in place until the limit is the creative decision's retry delay. It does not protect a sibling another process is writing.

The structural change worth weighing: publish the backup name only after `linked` is durable. Link the original to a partial name in the same directory, fsync the directory, write `linked`, `os.replace` the partial name onto `warehouse.issue147-swap.duckdb`, then `os.replace` the sibling onto `warehouse.duckdb`. A crash before the record is `linked` never occupies the backup name. Deleting the record is then enough to retry. The plan links straight to `warehouse.issue147-swap.duckdb` and writes `linked` afterward. A crash in between leaves `started`, a backup whose inode matches, and the sibling. An expired `started` entry stays final and does not replace, and every later `os.link` fails until a person removes both the record and that backup. Finish-swap can keep its current rule for a durable `linked` record.

That finish-swap still replaces when the original inode grew after the crash. Writers can commit into it once the flock is released. Those rows stay on the backup name and are no longer `warehouse.duckdb`. Comparing `st_size` to the stored `original_bytes` before the replace would refuse that swap. The plan does not do this, and the rows are not deleted.
